# A disk that holds another system is never touched unless --erase and the
# sentence naming it are given; looking at it changes nothing either.
@sata
@blank-disk 64 foreign
@install-medium
$ install
~ ^  sata0 +64 MiB  "QEMU HARDDISK": partitions of another system \(1 partition, type 83\)$
~ would refuse; with --erase: erase it and install
$ install sata0
~ ^install: sata0 is not empty\. To erase everything on it: install sata0 --erase$
$ install sata0 --reinstall
~ --reinstall is for a disk with BeeCodersOs
$ install nosuchdisk
~ no disk called nosuchdisk
$ install sata0 --format
~ ^usage: install
$& install sata0 --erase
> yes
$wait
That is not the sentence: nothing was changed.
$& install sata0 --erase
>^C
$wait
That is not the sentence: nothing was changed.
@disk-untouched

# With the sentence it is erased and installed.
$& install sata0 --erase
> erase 64 MiB QEMU HARDDISK
$wait
Installed on sata0. Take out the install medium and type: reboot
@boot-disk
$ pwd
/data
